<section id="names">
  <h2><span class="pt">PART A</span> Stage names</h2>
  <p class="lede">The stage layer already has a one-home rule and a derivation. Table names and column
  names have neither — which is the whole complaint.</p>

  <div class="scroll"><table>
    <thead><tr><th>Name-space</th><th>Lives in</th><th>Derived by</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td><b>Stage names</b></td><td><span class="m">governance/pipeline-contract.yaml</span> → <span class="m">stages[].id</span></td><td><span class="m">stage_label()</span></td></tr>
      <tr class="fault"><td><span class="m">Table names</span></td><td>the schema itself</td><td class="sub">nothing — each was named by whoever wrote its migration</td></tr>
      <tr class="fault"><td><span class="m">Key names</span></td><td>column names</td><td class="sub">nothing</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table></div>

  <h3>A.1 — The five ids</h3>
  <pre>STAGES = ["research", "evidence-collection", "judgment", "synthesis", "render"]

def stage_label(stage_id: str) -> str:      # tools/pipeline_completeness.py:42
    return stage_id.replace("-", " ")</pre>
  <p><b>Substrate is not in that list and must not be added to it.</b> It is the layer all five stages
  point into.</p>

  <h3>A.2 — The proposed prefix, derived rather than invented</h3>
  <p>An abbreviation table (<span class="m">research → res_</span>) would be a <em>second home for the
  stage name</em> — the exact defect rule 5 exists to stop. So the prefix must be <em>computed</em>
  from the id, the same way the display label is:</p>
  <pre>def stage_prefix(stage_id: str) -> str:     # one line, one home
    return stage_id[:3] + "_"</pre>
  <div class="scroll"><table>
    <thead><tr><th>Contract id</th><th>Display label</th><th>Prefix</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td><span class="m">research</span></td><td>research</td><td><span class="chip st-research">res_</span></td></tr>
      <tr><td><span class="m">evidence-collection</span></td><td>evidence collection</td><td><span class="chip st-evidence">evi_</span></td></tr>
      <tr><td><span class="m">judgment</span></td><td>judgment</td><td><span class="chip st-judgment">jud_</span></td></tr>
      <tr><td><span class="m">synthesis</span></td><td>synthesis</td><td><span class="chip st-synthesis">syn_</span></td></tr>
      <tr><td><span class="m">render</span></td><td>render</td><td><span class="chip st-render">ren_</span></td></tr>
      <tr><td colspan="2" class="sub">substrate — not a stage</td><td><span class="sub">no prefix</span></td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table></div>
  <p>All five are distinct at three characters. <b>Substrate takes no prefix, and the absence is the
  signal</b> — <em>no prefix means not a stage.</em> That is also the cheap answer: it leaves
  <span class="m">items</span>, <span class="m">populations</span>, <span class="m">slugs</span> and
  <span class="m">terms</span> — the four most-referenced names in the repository — untouched.</p>
</section>

<section id="keys">
  <h2><span class="pt">PART B</span> Keys, and what actually crosses a boundary</h2>
  <p class="lede">41 foreign keys cross a stage boundary; 39 stay inside one. Every one of the 41
  lands on one of seven columns. That is the entire cross-stage surface.</p>

  <div class="scroll"><table>
    <thead><tr><th>Target column</th><th>Stage</th><th class="num">Inbound</th><th>Who points at it</th></tr></thead>
