<section id="faults">
  <h2><span class="pt">PART C</span> Nine naming faults, each measured</h2>
  <p class="lede">Numbered because Part E cites them by number — the sequence carries information.</p>
  <ol class="faults">
    <li><b>No name carries its stage.</b> 0 of 66 tables are prefixed. Rule 5 is unusable without
      knowing a table's stage, and deriving that map is a <em>stopper</em> in CLAUDE.md. The name is
      where the derivation should already live.</li>
    <li><b>"Locator" means two opposite things in one schema.</b>
      <span class="m">source_locators</span> holds <span class="m">doi, url, pmid, pmcid, isbn, issn,
      standard_number</span> — <em>bibliographic identifiers</em>. Meanwhile R3 defines a locator as a
      within-document pointer (<em>"clause/section/page"</em>), which is what
      <span class="m">source_value_extractions</span>' sixteen <span class="m">loc_*</span> columns
      hold. The table named <span class="m">_locators</span> holds the sense it is not.</li>
    <li><b><span class="m">jurisdictional_values</span> holds no values.</b> 109 rows;
      <span class="m">value_text</span> and <span class="m">value_numeric</span> are <b>0 non-null</b>,
      by the REFERENCE-ONLY ruling of 2026-08-12. The name states the opposite of the ruling.</li>
    <li><b><span class="m">bpc_metadata</span> is not metadata.</b> It <em>is</em> the best-practice
      synthesis — the stage's output. <span class="m">BPC</span> is also an unexpanded acronym living
      in a table name, two columns and a junction.</li>
    <li><b>Two tables named <span class="m">*_specs</span> do not reference specifications.</b>
      <span class="m">case_study_specs.item_code → items.item_code</span> and
      <span class="m">economics_entry_specs.item_code → items.item_code</span>. Named for the judgment
      object, keyed on the render rollup.</li>
    <li><b>Three suffixes for one relation kind.</b> <span class="m">_links</span> ×9,
      <span class="m">_map</span> ×3, and seven bare-noun junctions. All are the same thing: a primary
      key composed of references to two other things.</li>
    <li><b>Head noun is plural in 52 tables and singular in 14.</b></li>
    <li><b>The activity is named, not the row.</b> <span class="m">citation_mining</span>,
      <span class="m">gap_mining</span>, <span class="m">supersession_check</span> name a
      <em>process</em>; one row is a <em>run of it</em>. Compare
      <span class="m">url_verification_runs</span>, which gets this right.</li>
    <li><b>Retired vocabulary is still in four table names and six columns.</b> Already ruled — the
      §R8 rename, then the register entry.</li>
  </ol>
</section>

<section id="grammar">
  <h2><span class="pt">PART D</span> The proposed grammar</h2>
  <div class="panel" style="border-left:3px solid var(--accent)">
    <p style="font-family:var(--sans);font-size:19px;font-weight:600;margin:0;max-width:none">
    <span style="color:var(--accent)">&lt;stage-prefix&gt;</span>
    &nbsp;<span>&lt;subject&gt;</span>&nbsp;
    <span style="color:var(--ink-2)">&lt;kind-suffix&gt;</span>
    <span style="font-weight:400;font-size:15px;color:var(--ink-3)">— head noun always plural</span></p>
  </div>
  <p><b>Prefix</b> — <span class="m">stage_id[:3] + "_"</span>, derived. No prefix means substrate.</p>
  <p><b>Kind suffix</b> — a closed set, each decided by a test against the schema itself rather than
  by taste:</p>
  <div class="scroll"><table>
    <thead><tr><th>Kind</th><th>Test</th><th>Suffix</th><th>Example</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td><b>registry</b></td><td>PK is a code this table mints, and other tables key to it</td><td class="sub">none</td><td><span class="m">items</span>, <span class="m">populations</span></td></tr>
      <tr><td><b>junction</b></td><td>PK composed of ≥2 columns that each identify <em>another thing</em> — a foreign key, or an external vocabulary with no table</td><td><span class="m">_links</span></td><td><span class="m">evi_slug_links</span></td></tr>
      <tr><td><b>run</b></td><td>a record of an act performed, carrying a timestamp and an outcome</td><td><span class="m">_runs</span></td><td><span class="m">evi_url_verification_runs</span></td></tr>
      <tr><td><b>record</b></td><td>anything else — one row is a thing that happened or was decided</td><td class="sub">plural noun naming what one row <em>is</em></td><td><span class="m">res_searches</span>, <span class="m">jud_specifications</span></td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table></div>
  <p class="note">The junction test is written against key columns rather than payload, because the
  payload test fails on real tables. <span class="m">term_aliases</span> has PK
  <span class="m">(term_id, alias, language)</span> and looks composite — but <span class="m">alias</span>
  <em>is</em> the payload, so the row is a record, not a junction, and it keeps its name.</p>
</section>

